<exercises xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" component="proteus-sandbox" xml:id="proteus-6-4">
<title>PROTEUS exercises</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The following questions ask you to recall some important ideas we have recently studied in preceding sections and then reflect on what these ideas have in common.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <exercise xml:id="proteus-6-4-MC-1" label="proteus-6-4-MC-1">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose we have a region bounded by a nonnegative function <m>y = f(x)</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis on an interval <m>[a, b]</m> and that <m>F(x)</m> is an antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m>.  If we want take a representative thin rectangular slice of the bounded area that has width <m>\Delta x</m>, what is the area of the representative slice?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A_{\text{slice}} \approx \int_a^b f(x) \, dx</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A_{\text{slice}} \approx f(x) \Delta x</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A_{\text{slice}} \approx f'(x) \Delta x</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A_{\text{slice}} \approx F(x) \Delta x</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="proteus-6-4-MC-2" label="proteus-6-4-MC-2">
        <statement>
            <p>
                In the same context where we have a region bounded by a nonnegative function <m>y = f(x)</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis on an interval <m>[a, b]</m>, and <m>F(x)</m> is an antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m>, what calculus expression provides the exact area of the region?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \int_a^b f(x) \, dx</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = f(x) \Delta x</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \int_a^b F(x) \, dx</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \int_a^b f'(x) \, dx</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="proteus-6-4-MC-3" label="proteus-6-4-MC-3">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose we know the nonnegative (and changing) velocity <m>v(t)</m> of a moving object on the time interval <m>[a, b]</m> and we wish to know the total distance the object travels.  Recall that when the nonnegative velocity of a moving object is <em>constant</em>,  <m>d = r \cdot t</m>.  In the usual way, let <m>a(t)</m> and <m>s(t)</m> be the corresponding acceleration and position functions that satisfy <m>a(t) = v'(t)</m> and <m>s'(t) = v(t)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                On a short time interval <m>\Delta t</m>, <m>v(t)</m> is roughly constant, so for a small slice of time, the corresponding small amount of distance traveled will be
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>d_{\text{slice}} \approx \int_a^b v(t) \, dt</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p><m>d_{\text{slice}} \approx v(t) \Delta t</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>d_{\text{slice}} \approx a(t) \Delta t</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A_{\text{slice}} \approx s(t) \Delta t</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="proteus-6-4-MC-4" label="proteus-6-4-MC-4">
        <statement>
            <p>
                In the same context where we have a nonnegative function <m>v(t)</m> on an interval <m>[a, b]</m>, and <m>s(t)</m> is the corresponding position function, what calculus expression provides the distance traveled?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p><m>D = \int_a^b v(t) \, dt</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>D = v(t) \Delta t</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>D = \int_a^b s(t) \, dt</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p><m>D = s(b)-s(a)</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise xml:id="proteus-6-4-MC-5" label="proteus-6-4-MC-5">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Recall that for an object with constant density, <m>D</m>, mass equals density times volume, or <me>M = D \cdot V</me>. If we want to determine the mass of an object that has non-constant density, we have to use calculus in order to find its mass.  For example, suppose we have a thin rod of length <m>(b-a)</m> lying on the <m>x</m>-axis from <m>x=a</m> to <m>x=a</m> whose cross sections have constant density, but whose density is distributed according to the function <m>\rho(x)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                On a short interval of length <m>\Delta x</m>, <m>\rho(x)</m> is roughly constant, so for a small slice of the rod, the corresponding small amount of mass will be
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>M_{\text{slice}} \approx \int_a^b \rho(x) \, dx</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p><m>M_{\text{slice}} \approx \rho(x) \Delta x</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>M_{\text{slice}} \approx \rho'(x) \Delta x</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>M_{\text{slice}} \approx \rho(x) (b-a))</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="proteus-6-4-MC-6" label="proteus-6-4-MC-6">
        <statement>
            <p>
                In the same context where we have a nonconstant continuous density function <m>\rho(x)</m> on an interval <m>[a, b]</m>, what calculus expression provides the exact mass, <m>M</m>, of the rod?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p><m>M = \int_a^b \rho(x) \, dx</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>M = \rho(x) \Delta x</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>M = \frac{1}{b-a} \int_a^b \rho(x) \, dx</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>M = \rho(b)-\rho(a)</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>(feedback)</p>
                </feedback>    
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="short-answer-question">
        <statement>
             <p> How are all three of these situations <mdash/> finding area, finding distance traveled, finding total mass<mdash/> similar?  What general pattern(s) do you notice?</p>
        </statement>
        <response/>
    </exercise>

</exercises>
